    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green (36 tests, 0 failures, BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country validation of the E.164 national-number length in the owner-creation/update flow.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:\n\n1. Added a constant mapping recognised country codes to their required national-number length:\n   ```java\n   private static final Map<String, Integer> NATIONAL_NUMBER_LENGTHS = Map.of(\"61\", 9, \"1\", 10);\n   ```\n2. In `toE164(...)`, after the existing generic 8\u201315 total-digit check, added a call to a new helper `hasValidNationalNumberLength(digits)`; when the length is wrong the method returns `null`, which the controller already maps to a **400 Bad Request**.\n3. `hasValidNationalNumberLength` picks the longest matching country code and requires the digits after it to number exactly what that country needs. Country codes not in the map (e.g. `+64`) fall through to the generic rule, preserving existing behaviour.\n4. Added the `java.util.Comparator` import and updated the Javadoc.\n\n**Behaviour**\n- `+61 123` \u2192 national number `123` (3 digits, needs 9) \u2192 rejected 400 (Cp26). \u2713\n- `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678` (9 national digits) \u2192 accepted (Cp02/Cp08). \u2713\n- `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456`, unrecognised country code \u2192 generic rule \u2192 accepted (Cp08). \u2713\n- A `+1` number must carry 10 national digits.\n\nAll 36 tests pass, including the target Cp26 and the existing telephone tests (Cp02, Cp03, Cp08).",
